WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.480
Two Indian startups will test orbital computing and thermal sensing on a falcon 9 tonight. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 117. Take me to space is sending a 14 kilogram satellite called MOI-1A on tonight's transporter 18 mission. The spacecraft carries 117 trillion operations per second of onboard computing, two terabytes of storage, and a 9 band multispectral imager.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:34.420
It will run customer AI models directly in orbit and beam back only the processed results

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.580
for uses such as agricultural monitoring and supply chain work.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.700
Sat Leah Labs is flying Topos 1 India's first commercial Cube Sat thermal payload on

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:46.480
the same rocket.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:52.100
The launch window opens at 11.48 pm India Standard Time from Vandenberg.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.900
Take me to space already tested on orbit inference with a smaller demonstration satellite

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.500
in December 2024. The company now aims for a six satellite constellation that would give daily

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.680
global coverage by the fourth quarter of 2027. SpaceX also laid out details for its first orbital

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.760
data center satellite. The AI-1 spacecraft is designed to average 120 kilowatts of compute power

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:25.400
using large solar arrays and radiators. The satellites will be built at a new gigasat factory in

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:31.820
in Bastrop, Texas. Space X has filed with the FCC for a constellation of up to 1 million

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:37.600
such satellites. The company wants 5 to 10 gigawatts of terrestrial data center capacity

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:44.460
in place by 2027, before moving workloads into orbit. Elon Musk noted that once launch

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.420
costs fall far enough, abundant solar power in space makes orbital AI compute attractive.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:56.560
Starship Flight 14 reached orbit and deployed 26 Starlink version 3 satellites,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.740
despite one upper-stage Raptor shutting down early. Those satellites add roughly 26

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.900
terabytes per second of capacity. A fully mature Starship could carry up to 60

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:13.640
version 3 satellites on a single flight. Flight 15 is now targeted no earlier than

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.540
October 19th from Orbital Launchpad 2 at Starbase. The stack will be booster

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:25.780
22 and ship 42. The main goal is the first attempted tower catch of a starship

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:31.020
upper stage. The same flight will also begin testing on or a bit propellant transfer

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:36.200
docking and hatch operations. SpaceX has three launches scheduled across both

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:43.380
coasts today. Crew 13 lifts off at 11.10 a.m. Eastern from SLC 40 on a Falcon 9

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.660
carrying four astronauts to the ISS.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:52.700
Transporter 18 follows from Vandenberg carrying 130 payloads, including a Google Project

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:53.980
Suncatcher prototype.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:00.720
The day closes with a Falcon Heavy launching the classified NRL 97 payload from Kennedy

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.760
Space Center at 1153 PM Eastern.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:10.460
The only prior triple launch inside 13 hours happened on March 14th and 15th of 2025.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:16.620
NASA plans an uncrewed Starliner 1 flight no later than January 2027.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.500
That mission will validate fixes before a crewed Starliner 2 flight aimed at 2028.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.920
The agency expects up to 5 crewed Starliner missions to the ISS overall.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.360
Administrator Jared Isaacman said NASA and Boeing have addressed the issues from earlier

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:33.820
flights.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:42.880
The Starlink constellation now stands at 12,962 satellites launched and 11,119 operational.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.300
12 satellites carry active reentry predictions.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.020
One conjunction shows on the SOCRATI's list, though the tracking data behind it is stale.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:57.840
Needham kept its buy rating on SPCX with a $250 target.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:04.640
The firm sees potential for a $54 billion annualized revenue run rate from AI compute contracts.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:13.900
Analyst Jean Munster projects 170-180 missions this year, scaling toward 2,200 annual launches by 2030.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.720
U.S. government sales made up 20.9% of SpaceX revenue in 2025.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.960
That slice includes national security work through Starsheald.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.680
Export controls still limit some products and services.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:35.120
RGB aerial photography posted a time lapse of Starship Flight 14 that shows pre-dawn fueling

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.540
and liftoff at Starbase.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.760
Observers noted the sequence now feels routine enough to appear in short social posts before

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:43.700
most people wake.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.980
On Reddit, users asked whether current Starship vehicles include crew quarters and when human

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.500
rating hardware might arrive.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:57.520
The monthly discussion thread also opened on the SpaceX Lounge subreddit for any SpaceX

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.000
or spaceflight questions.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:05.440
One thing worth watching is short-seller Jim Chainos highlighting an 80% cut in Musk's

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.800
2027 Starship launch forecast.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.960
The target moved from daily flights to 50-100 launches annualized.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:19.460
Consultant Tim Ferrar argued the revised cadence may represent only a year and goal rather

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.560
than a sustained rate.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:27.380
On the AI front, SpaceX plans to scale AI workload capacity into space after acquiring

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.900
XAI in February 2026.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.760
Heat rejection in vacuum remains a key physics challenge, though Musk noted Starlink's

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.660
10,000 satellites already demonstrate reliable radiator performance.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:46.460
Needham estimates SpaceX AI compute deals could reach a $54 billion annualized revenue

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.880
run rate based on disclosed contracts.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:54.180
The engineering reality behind orbital data centers begins with power and heat.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.960
In vacuum, the only heat transfer mechanism is radiation.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.440
So radiator area scales directly with the kilowatts that must be rejected.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.040
Starlings existing 10,000 satellites already fly comparable radiator designs,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.400
giving SpaceX flight heritage on the thermal side.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.040
The economic lever is launch cost.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:20.140
cost. One Starship drops the price per kilogram low enough. The marginal cost of placing a kilowatt

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:26.680
hour of compute in orbit falls below terrestrial equivalents that must also pay for land, cooling

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:32.800
towers, and grid connections. Musk's stated target of 5 to 10 gigawatts of terrestrial

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:40.140
capacity by 2027 therefore serves as a bridge, letting the company amortize AI1 production tooling

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.100
while Starship Cadence improves.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.020
If the first orbital units reach 120 kilowatts each, and the constellation scales, total

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:54.160
on-orbit compute could eventually exceed any single terrestrial cluster without requiring

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:55.660
new power plants on the ground.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:01.720
The design choice to fly large solar arrays paired with dedicated radiators mirrors the

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:08.040
thermal architecture already proven on Starlink, reducing the number of new failure modes, production

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.120
at the Bastrop Gigasat factory will need to achieve high throughput to support even a modest

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:20.500
initial constellation before the million satellite FCC filing becomes relevant. The 120 kilowatt

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:25.640
average power figure sets a clear floor for radiator sizing and solar array output,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.960
two variables that must and balance across every orbit. Once Starship reaches routine weekly

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:37.480
flights, the cost per kilogram delivered drops far enough that the raw materials and silicon

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:42.080
on inside each satellite become the dominant expense rather than the ride to orbit.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.340
That shift is the exact point at which orbital data centers move from conceptual to economically

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.500
competitive with ground-based clusters.

